- Use Mermaid for creating maintainable diagrams and visualizations as code.
- Choose appropriate diagram types (flowchart, sequence, class, ERD) for different use cases.
- Use clear and descriptive node labels and relationship descriptions.
- Apply consistent styling and theming for professional diagram appearance.
- Structure complex diagrams with proper grouping and subgraphs for clarity.
- Use appropriate arrow types and line styles to convey different relationships.
- Implement proper version control for diagrams by storing them as text.
- Include diagrams in documentation workflows with automated rendering.
- Use Mermaid live editor for rapid prototyping and collaborative diagram design.
- Export diagrams to various formats (SVG, PNG, PDF) for different documentation needs.

## Overview
Enforces NVIDIA branding standards for all Mermaid diagrams in TensorRT-LLM documentation.

## Core Requirements

### Mandatory Color Palette
- **NVIDIA Green**: #76b900 (Primary elements, success states, lightweight components)
- **NVIDIA Blue**: #0071c5 (Processing steps, active elements, key metrics)
- **NVIDIA Gray**: #5e5e5e (Secondary information, disabled states)
- **NVIDIA Black**: #000000 (Heavy components, GPU memory, critical paths)
- **NVIDIA Light Gray**: #cdcdcd (Neutral elements, backgrounds, communication flows)

### Required Theme Configuration
All Mermaid diagrams MUST include this theme initialization:

```mermaid
%%{init: {
    'theme': 'base',
    'themeVariables': {
        'primaryColor': '#76b900',
        'primaryTextColor': '#fff',
        'primaryBorderColor': '#000',
        'lineColor': '#000',
        'secondaryColor': '#0071c5',
        'tertiaryColor': '#5e5e5e',
        'background': '#fff',
        'mainBkg': '#76b900',
        'secondBkg': '#0071c5',
        'tertiaryBkg': '#5e5e5e'
    }
}}%%
```

### Required Class Definitions
All diagrams MUST include these NVIDIA color classes:

```mermaid
classDef nvidiaGreen fill:#76b900,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
classDef nvidiaBlue fill:#0071c5,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
classDef nvidiaGray fill:#5e5e5e,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
classDef nvidiaBlack fill:#000,stroke:#fff,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
classDef nvidiaLightGray fill:#cdcdcd,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px,color:#000
```

### Color Assignment Guidelines
- **Green**: Primary elements, success states, lightweight components (router hash tables)
- **Blue**: Processing steps, active elements, key metrics
- **Gray**: Secondary information, disabled states, what's NOT stored
- **Black**: Heavy components, GPU memory, critical paths
- **Light Gray**: Neutral elements, backgrounds, communication flows

### Accessibility Requirements
- Ensure text contrast meets WCAG AA standards
- Use patterns or shapes in addition to color for critical distinctions
- Provide text descriptions for color-coded elements
- Test diagrams in both light and dark modes

### Quality Standards
- Use colors consistently across related diagrams
- Always include class definitions in every diagram
- Follow TensorRT-LLM documentation standards
- Ensure sufficient contrast for readability

## Best Practices
- Use Method 2 (Class-Based Styling) for maximum control
- Apply NVIDIA branding consistently across all technical documentation
- Test diagram readability in various viewing contexts
- Maintain color consistency across diagram series

## Example Implementation
```mermaid
%%{init: {'theme': 'base', 'themeVariables': {'primaryColor': '#76b900'}}}%%
graph TB
    A["TensorRT Engine"]
    B["GPU Memory"]
    
    classDef nvidiaGreen fill:#76b900,stroke:#000,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
    classDef nvidiaBlack fill:#000,stroke:#fff,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
    
    class A nvidiaGreen
    class B nvidiaBlack
    
    A --> B
``` 

